---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// The 23 things build() does, in source order (core/ReelSetBuilder.ts:968-1440).
// Drawn as three figures of eight, eight and seven steps; the step number is
// part of the title so the table further down can refer to it.
type Kind = 'primary' | 'accent' | 'subtle' | 'muted' | 'ghost' | 'code';
const STEPS: [string, string[], Kind][] = [
  ['_validate()', ['every error collected', 'one combined throw'], 'accent'],
  ['single throws', ['direction / curve lengths', 'warp renderer, bufferEnd 0'], 'accent'],
  ['axis projection', ['reelAxis(orientation, fwd)', 'main / cross cell and gap'], 'subtle'],
  ['cells and extents', ['maxCells | perReel | uniform', 'tallest = max(extents)'], 'subtle'],
  ['speed default', ['normal = SpeedPresets.NORMAL', 'only if none registered'], 'subtle'],
  ['symbolsData', ['weight ?? 10, zIndex ?? 1', 'unmask, size per id'], 'subtle'],
  ['pool capacity', ['?? max(20, strip cells)'], 'subtle'],
  ['new SymbolFactory', ['registry, capacity,', 'gsap, axis mainProp'], 'code'],
  ['random provider', ['new RandomSymbolProvider', 'then set(pool, scope) each'], 'code'],
  ['new FrameBuilder', ['over the random provider', 'use(mw) in call order'], 'code'],
  ['tumble phases', ['cascade:fall/place/dropIn', 'only after .tumble()'], 'subtle'],
  ['MultiWays adjust', ['registerFactory(adjust)', 'AdjustPhase(duration, ease)'], 'subtle'],
  ['user .phases()', ['runs last', 'override always wins'], 'accent'],
  ['viewport size', ['crossSpan x tallest', 'through setAxis.toScreen'], 'subtle'],
  ['mask auto-pick', ['Rect or SharedRect', 'warp-skips-unmask notice'], 'subtle'],
  ['curve focus', ['reel 0 / lean .5 / set 1', 'setCentreCross = span / 2'], 'subtle'],
  ['new ReelViewport', ['w, h, strategy, axis, bleed', 'bounding-box mask for now'], 'code'],
  ['initial frame', ['assertBufferCountsInRange', 'coordinateBigSymbols()'], 'subtle'],
  ['closure capture', ['builder fields -> consts', 'perReel(), mainOffsetFor()'], 'subtle'],
  ['createReel x N', ['frameBuilder.build(seed)', 'new Reel, referenceSpeed'], 'code'],
  ['updateMaskSize', ['one maskRectFor(i) per reel'], 'subtle'],
  ['ReelFactory', ['create, extentFor, maskRect,', 'mainOffset, poolCapacity'], 'subtle'],
  ['new ReelSet', ['config, reels, factory,', 'viewport, pools, modes'], 'primary'],
];
const COLS = 3;
const BW = 220;
const BH = 78;
const GX = 20;
const PITCH_Y = 122;
const X0 = 10;
const Y0 = 20;
const pos = (i: number) => ({ x: X0 + (i % COLS) * (BW + GX), y: Y0 + Math.floor(i / COLS) * PITCH_Y });
const figureHeight = (n: number) => Y0 + Math.ceil(n / COLS) * PITCH_Y - (PITCH_Y - BH) + 20;
const FIGURES: [number, number, string][] = [
  [0, 8, 'Steps 1 to 8: the two gates, the derived numbers, and the first engine object. Validation throws once, before anything is allocated.'],
  [8, 16, 'Steps 9 to 16: the frame pipeline, the phase factories (your .phases() calls run last) and the viewport geometry.'],
  [16, 23, 'Steps 17 to 23: the viewport, one reel per index, the factory that addReels() reuses later, and finally the ReelSet that owns them all.'],
];
---
<Architecture
  title="The builder"
  subtitle="What build() validates and constructs, in order."
  eyebrow="Construction"
>
  <p>
    Before a reel set can spin it needs to know a surprising number of
    things: how many columns there are, how many rows each column shows, how
    big a cell is, which symbols exist and how each one is drawn, which ticker
    drives the animation, and a long tail of optional details about curves,
    masks, gaps and timing. Many of those settings only make sense together.
    A symbol that spans two columns needs a mask that does not clip the gap
    between them. A curve drawn in "warp" mode needs a renderer to draw with.
    A list of per-reel values has to be exactly as long as the number of
    reels. If the library checked each value the moment you set it, it would
    have to guess at the values you had not set yet, and the first sign of a
    mistake would be a blank board on the first spin.
  </p>

  <p>
    The builder solves this by splitting setup into two halves. While you
    call setters, it only writes values down. When you call <code>build()</code>, it checks everything at once, constructs the engine
    in a fixed order, and hands you back the <code>ReelSet</code>. The rule
    written in the source header is that the builder "throws at construction,
    not at first spin": a mistake surfaces as one error listing every problem,
    before a single symbol has been drawn.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    Think of the builder as a form you fill in, in any order, and <code>build()</code> as the submit button. Nothing is validated and
    nothing is allocated until you press it. The class is <code>ReelSetBuilder</code> in <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelSetBuilder.ts</code>.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The five things you must say</h2>

  <p>
    Every setter on the builder is optional except five. The table lists them
    in the order most people write them, but the order is free: each setter
    returns the builder, so you chain them however reads best.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Call</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">What it tells the builder</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['.reels(n)', 'how many columns. pixi-reels calls a column a reel even when the set runs horizontally.'],
          ['.visibleCells(n), .visibleCellsPerReel([...]) or .multiways({...})', 'how many cells each reel shows inside the window. Exactly one of the three.'],
          ['.symbolSize(w, h)', 'the size of one cell, in screen pixels.'],
          ['.symbols(cb)', 'a callback that registers every symbol id together with the class that draws it.'],
          ['.ticker(ticker)', 'the PixiJS ticker that drives every animation in the set.'],
        ].map(([c, w]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">{c}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80">{w}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    One ordering rule is worth knowing before anything else. A spin is made of
    stages called phases (start, spin, stop and so on), and <code>.phases()</code> lets you override how one of them behaves. The
    builder does not apply that override where it sits in your chain; it
    saves it and applies it at the very end of <code>build()</code>, after the
    default phases that tumble and MultiWays sets install. So your overrides
    always win, whether you write them first or last.
  </p>

  <h2>What build() does, in order</h2>

  <p>
    <code>build()</code> runs twenty-three steps in a fixed sequence. Figures
    1 to 3 draw them in source order, read left to right and row by row. Look
    at the colours: the highlighted boxes near the start are gates that throw
    or decide and allocate nothing, the dark boxes are the moments an engine
    object is constructed, and the single primary box at the very end is the <code>ReelSet</code> itself. Everything that can fail does so in the first
    two steps, before anything exists that would need cleaning up.
  </p>

  {FIGURES.map(([start, end, caption]) => (
    <Diagram height={figureHeight(end - start)} caption={caption}>
      {STEPS.slice(start, end).map(([title, sub, kind], j) => {
        const { x, y } = pos(j);
        return <Box x={x} y={y} w={BW} h={BH} title={`${start + j + 1}. ${title}`} sub={sub} kind={kind} />;
      })}
      {STEPS.slice(start, end).map((_, j) => {
        if (j === end - start - 1) return null;
        const a = pos(j);
        const b = pos(j + 1);
        if (j % COLS < COLS - 1) {
          return <Edge from={[a.x + BW, a.y + BH / 2]} to={[b.x, b.y + BH / 2]} />;
        }
        const midY = a.y + BH + (PITCH_Y - BH) / 2;
        return <Edge from={[a.x + BW / 2, a.y + BH]} via={[[a.x + BW / 2, midY], [b.x + BW / 2, midY]]} to={[b.x + BW / 2, b.y]} />;
      })}
    </Diagram>
  ))}

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'Gate', note: 'throws or decides; nothing is allocated yet' },
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'Computation', note: 'derived numbers and tables' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'new X', note: 'dark boxes construct an engine object' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'Result', note: 'the ReelSet you add to the stage' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <h2>The steps, in groups</h2>

  <p>
    Twenty-three steps are easier to hold as seven groups. Each paragraph
    below says what a group does and why it comes where it does; the exact
    formulas are collected in the table that follows, so the prose can stay
    readable. A few words first, since the rest of the page leans on them. The <em>main axis</em> is the direction a reel travels (down the screen on a
    vertical set, across it on a horizontal one) and the <em>cross axis</em> is the other one. A reel's <em>extent</em> is how long it is along the
    main axis, in pixels. A <em>strip</em> is the array of symbols a reel
    holds, which is longer than the window because of the <em>buffer
    cells</em> parked just outside it on either end.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 1 and 2, the gates.</strong> The first step walks every
    validation rule, collects every message, and throws once with the header <code>ReelSetBuilder validation failed:</code> followed by one <code>- message</code> line per problem, so you fix a bad configuration in
    one round trip rather than one error at a time. The full list of messages
    is further down the page. Four checks are not folded into that list and
    throw on their own: the length of <code>directionPerReel()</code>, the
    length of <code>curvePerReel()</code>, warp curve mode without a
    renderer, and a zero end buffer on a set that is not a tumble set.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 3 and 4, the geometry.</strong> The builder makes a set-level
    axis object from the orientation and uses it to translate every later
    size into screen x and y, which is why the same code lays out vertical and
    horizontal sets. On a vertical set the main cell size is the symbol height
    and the cross cell size is the symbol width; on a horizontal set they
    swap, and the gaps swap with them. It then works out how many cells each
    reel shows and how long each reel is, and records the tallest extent,
    because every shorter reel will be positioned inside that tallest box.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 5 to 8, speed and symbols.</strong> If you registered no
    speed profile, the builder installs the normal preset so a set always has
    one; the first profile's spin speed becomes the reference speed each reel
    is handed later. It then builds a metadata record for every registered
    symbol id (weight, draw order, whether it may escape the mask, and its
    size in cells) with the defaults filled in. Symbols are pooled rather
    than allocated per spin, so the builder sizes the pool next: unless you
    set a capacity, it is large enough for every strip to show one id at
    once, with a floor of twenty. With those in hand it constructs the <code>SymbolFactory</code>, the object that hands out and takes back
    symbol instances. The <a href="/architecture/symbols/">symbols page</a> has the pool in detail.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 9 and 10, the frame pipeline.</strong> A <em>frame</em> is
    the list of symbol ids a reel will show after it lands, buffers included.
    The builder constructs the random provider that fills cells nobody has
    claimed, applies every random-draw pool and scope you registered, and
    then wraps it in the <code>FrameBuilder</code>, adding your frame
    middleware in the order you called it, after the two built-in stages. The <a href="/architecture/frame-pipeline/">frame pipeline page</a> explains
    how a result grid becomes a frame.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 11 to 13, the phases.</strong> Two features ship phases of
    their own. A tumble set (where winning symbols are removed and the ones
    above fall into the holes) gets three cascade phases: fall, place and
    drop-in. Gravity is deliberately left unresolved here and read per reel at
    runtime, because reels can run in different directions. A MultiWays set
    (where the server changes the number of cells per reel on each spin) gets
    an adjust phase that migrates pinned cells, with a duration that may be a
    per-reel function. Only after both defaults does the builder run your <code>.phases()</code> callbacks, so the last registration per phase key
    is always yours.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 14 to 17, the viewport.</strong> The viewport is the window
    you see the reels through. Its size is the cross span of all reels by the
    tallest extent. The builder then chooses a mask (figure 4 below), computes
    how strongly a curve leans towards the centre of the set, and constructs
    the <code>ReelViewport</code>. In warp curve mode it also passes the <em>bleed</em>, an extra margin on the cross axis so a bent reel is not
    clipped at its edges. At this point no reel exists yet, so the viewport
    has no per-reel rectangles to mask with; it draws a bounding-box mask as a
    stand-in until step 21 replaces it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 18 to 21, the reels.</strong> If you supplied an initial
    frame, it is checked against the buffer counts and any big symbols are
    laid out now, so a block that does not fit throws before any reel exists.
    Every builder field the reel code needs is then copied into local
    constants (the section on the closure rule explains why), along with two
    helpers: <code>perReel()</code>, which reads a per-reel array and reuses
    its last entry for any index past the end, and <code>mainOffsetFor()</code>, which positions a short reel inside the
    tallest box according to the anchor you chose. The loop then creates one
    reel per index: it asks the frame builder for a seed strip, assembles the
    reel's config, constructs the <code>Reel</code> (which adds its own
    container to the viewport's masked layer), and sets the reel's reference
    speed before installing the drive, because a drive's profile-relative
    bounds resolve against that speed. When the loop ends, the viewport's mask
    is redrawn with one rectangle per reel.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Steps 22 and 23, the factory and the set.</strong> The reel-making
    code is bundled into a <code>ReelFactory</code>, an object of closures
    that <code>addReels()</code> and <code>expand()</code> call later, so a
    reel added at runtime is built exactly the way the originals were. Last of
    all the <code>ReelSet</code> is constructed with the config, the reels,
    the factory, the viewport, the symbol factory, the frame builder, the
    phase factory and the three modes (spinning, default spin and skip). The
    set builds its own speed manager, spin controller and spotlight; the <a href="/architecture/classes/">classes page</a> has that order.
  </p>

  <p>
    The formulas, for when you need the exact number.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Step</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Quantity</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">How it is derived</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['3', 'setAxis', "reelAxis(orientation, 'forward'); main cell = symbolHeight (vertical) or symbolWidth (horizontal), cross cell the other; gaps swap the same way; every later geometry number goes through setAxis.toScreen"],
          ['4', 'cells per reel', 'MultiWays: maxCells on every reel; else visibleCellsPerReel; else visibleCells on every reel'],
          ['4', 'extent per reel', 'MultiWays reelExtent, else reelExtents[i], else cells * mainCell + (cells - 1) * mainGap; tallest = max(extents)'],
          ['5', 'speed', 'no profile registered: normal = SpeedPresets.NORMAL; defaultSpinSpeed = the first profile\'s spinSpeed'],
          ['6', 'symbolsData[id]', 'weight = override.weight ?? weights[id] ?? 10; zIndex = override.zIndex ?? 1; unmask and size from symbolData()'],
          ['7', 'pool capacity', 'poolCapacity ?? max(20, sum over reels of (cells + bufferStart + bufferEnd))'],
          ['8', 'SymbolFactory', 'new SymbolFactory(registry, poolCapacity, gsap, setAxis.mainProp)'],
          ['9', 'random provider', 'new RandomSymbolProvider(symbolsData, rng), then set(pool, scope) for each .randomSymbols() call'],
          ['10', 'FrameBuilder', 'over the provider; use(mw) for each .frameMiddleware() in call order, after the two built-ins'],
          ['11', 'tumble phases', 'factories for cascade:fall, cascade:place, cascade:dropIn, only after .tumble()'],
          ['12', 'adjust phase', "registerFactory('adjust', ...) producing AdjustPhase(reel, speed, { durationMs, ease })"],
          ['14', 'viewport size', 'crossSpan = reelCount * (crossCell + crossGap) - crossGap; viewportSize = setAxis.toScreen(crossSpan, tallest)'],
          ['16', 'curve focus', 'curveFocusWeight = CURVE_FOCUS_WEIGHT[focus] (reel 0, set-lean 0.5, set 1); setCentreCross = crossSpan / 2'],
          ['17', 'ReelViewport', 'new ReelViewport(w, h, undefined, maskStrategy, setAxis, warp ? curveBleed : 0)'],
          ['18', 'initial frame', 'assertBufferCountsInRange(initialFrame, ...), then coordinateBigSymbols() gives one seed strip per reel'],
          ['19', 'perReel(values, i)', 'values[i], or the last entry when i is past the end'],
          ['19', 'mainOffsetFor(extent, tallest)', "'start' -> 0; 'end' -> tallest - extent; 'center' -> (tallest - extent) / 2"],
          ['20', 'cell main size', 'spinCellSize for MultiWays or when there are no explicit extents; otherwise (extent - (cells - 1) * mainGap) / cells'],
          ['20', 'seed strip', 'frameBuilder.build(i, cells, bufferStart, bufferEnd, seed)'],
          ['20', 'reel axis', 'reelAxis(orientation, perReel(directionPerReel) ?? direction)'],
          ['20', 'reel curveFocus', 'crossCell / 2 + w * (setCentreCross - i * (crossCell + crossGap) - crossCell / 2)'],
          ['20', 'the Reel', 'new Reel(config, symbolFactory, randomProvider, viewport); config also carries the per-reel curve, the warp renderer and ticker, bleed, stacking, gsap and symbolZIndex; reel.referenceSpeed = defaultSpinSpeed, then installDrive(drive)'],
          ['21', 'mask', 'viewport.updateMaskSize(w, h, maskRects), one rect per reel; the rect formula is on the viewport page'],
          ['22', 'ReelFactory', 'closures: extentFor, create (the same createReel), maskRect, mainOffset, viewportSize, poolCapacity'],
          ['23', 'ReelSet', 'new ReelSet with config, reels, reelFactory, viewport, symbolFactory, frameBuilder, phaseFactory, spinningMode, defaultSpinMode, skipMode'],
        ].map(([s, q, h]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{s}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">{q}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-foreground/80">{h}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Mask auto-pick</h2>

  <p>
    The mask is the shape that decides which part of each strip the player
    can see; everything outside it, buffer cells included, is clipped. The
    default mask is one rectangle per reel, which is exactly right for a
    plain grid and wrong for two situations: a symbol that is wider than one
    reel would be clipped in the gap between columns, and a reel that leans
    towards the centre of the set (a curve with a set-wide focus) would be
    clipped at its own column edge. In both cases the builder quietly switches
    to one shared rectangle around the whole set. Figure 4 shows the
    decision: the questions are asked top to bottom, an explicit strategy
    short-circuits everything, both automatic rules land on the shared
    rectangle, and the default is the per-reel rectangle.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={490} caption="How build() chooses the mask. Questions are asked top to bottom; an explicit strategy short-circuits everything, both auto rules land on SharedRect, and the default is Rect.">
    <Box x={20} y={20} w={280} h={78} title="explicit strategy?" kind="ghost" mono={false} sub={['.maskStrategy(s) was called', 's.version must be 2']} />
    <Box x={20} y={150} w={280} h={78} title="wide block or unmask?" kind="ghost" mono={false} sub={['size.reels > 1 or unmask: true', 'and the cross gap is > 0']} />
    <Box x={20} y={280} w={280} h={78} title="curve leans?" kind="ghost" mono={false} sub={["curveFocus is not 'reel'", 'and a curve is set']} />
    <Box x={20} y={410} w={280} h={60} title="RectMaskStrategy" sub="default: one rect per reel" />

    <Edge from={[160, 98]} to={[160, 150]} label="no" labelDx={30} />
    <Edge from={[160, 228]} to={[160, 280]} label="no" labelDx={30} />
    <Edge from={[160, 358]} to={[160, 410]} label="no" labelDx={30} />

    <Box x={420} y={29} w={280} h={60} title="your strategy" kind="accent" mono={false} sub="explicit always wins" />
    <Box x={420} y={159} w={280} h={60} title="SharedRectMaskStrategy" kind="accent" sub="notice: mask-auto-shared" />
    <Box x={420} y={289} w={280} h={60} title="SharedRectMaskStrategy" kind="accent" sub="notice: mask-auto-shared" />

    <Edge from={[300, 59]} to={[420, 59]} label="yes" labelDy={-24} />
    <Edge from={[300, 189]} to={[420, 189]} label="yes" labelDy={-24} />
    <Edge from={[300, 319]} to={[420, 319]} label="yes" labelDy={-24} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The first rule only fires when there is a gap between columns, because
    without a gap there is nothing to clip: a tall block that spans several
    cells in one reel never crosses a column edge, so it does not trigger the
    rule either. The second rule does not need a gap, because a set-focused
    curve leans receding cells out of their own column whether or not a gap
    is there. Both rules log an info notice named <code>mask-auto-shared</code> that says which reason fired, so you can
    tell from the console why your mask is not the default. The <a href="/architecture/viewport-and-masks/">viewport page</a> has every
    strategy you can choose explicitly.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    The shared rectangle has a cost on sets where reels differ in height: it
    uncovers the buffer cells beyond the short reels. If you combine a
    pyramid layout with wide symbols, either hide the peek behind frame art
    or pass a mask strategy of your own, which always wins over the auto-pick.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Every setter, grouped by concern</h2>

  <p>
    The reference, for when you need a default or a constraint. Each card
    groups the setters that affect one concern; required calls are marked.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Grid shape</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>reels(count)</code> required.</li>
        <li><code>visibleCells(n)</code> uniform window; cannot be combined with <code>visibleCellsPerReel</code>.</li>
        <li><code>visibleCellsPerReel([...])</code> jagged or pyramid; length must equal <code>reels()</code>.</li>
        <li><code>reelExtents([...])</code> per-reel main-axis pixel box; combined with <code>reelAnchor</code>, extents win.</li>
        <li><code>reelAnchor('start'|'center'|'end')</code> default <code>'center'</code>: where short reels sit inside the tallest.</li>
        <li><code>multiways({'{ minCells, maxCells, reelExtent }'})</code> per-spin shape; no big symbols, no <code>visibleCellsPerReel</code>.</li>
        <li><code>symbolSize(w, h)</code> required, screen space. <code>symbolGap(x, y)</code> default <code>{'{0, 0}'}</code>, screen space.</li>
        <li><code>bufferSymbols(n | {'{ start, end }'})</code> default <code>1 / 1</code>; values below 1 are clamped to 1 with a one-time <code>buffer-clamped</code> warning; <code>end: 0</code> only on tumble sets.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Orientation, direction, layering</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>orientation('vertical'|'horizontal')</code> default <code>'vertical'</code>.</li>
        <li><code>direction('forward'|'reverse')</code> default <code>'forward'</code>; <code>directionPerReel([...])</code> per reel.</li>
        <li><code>cellStacking(order)</code> default <code>'ascending'</code>; geometric, a reversed direction does not flip it.</li>
        <li><code>reelStacking(order)</code> default <code>'ascending'</code>, which draws the last reel in front.</li>
        <li><code>symbolZIndex(resolver)</code> default <code>null</code>; replaces <code>zIndex * 100 + cellIndex</code> as the only draw-order source.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mt-4 mb-1">Curvature</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>curve(input)</code>, <code>curvePerReel([...])</code>.</li>
        <li><code>curveFocus('reel'|'set-lean'|'set')</code> default <code>'reel'</code>; anything else auto-shares the mask.</li>
        <li><code>curveMode('symbol'|'warp')</code> default <code>'symbol'</code>; warp needs <code>renderer(app.renderer)</code>.</li>
        <li><code>curveBleed(px)</code> default <code>0</code>, warp only. See <a href="/architecture/curvature/">curvature</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Masking and motion</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>maskStrategy(s)</code> default <code>new RectMaskStrategy()</code>; rejects null, objects without <code>build</code>/<code>update</code>, and any <code>version !== 2</code>. Marks the choice explicit.</li>
        <li><code>motionModel('tween')</code> default, or <code>motionModel('drive', config)</code>; the config is validated by <code>assertDriveConfig</code> and there is no runtime toggle.</li>
        <li><code>speed(name, profile)</code>; <code>initialSpeed(name)</code> default <code>'normal'</code>.</li>
        <li><code>skipMode('slam'|'quicken')</code> default <code>'slam'</code>.</li>
        <li><code>pinMigrationDuration(ms | fn)</code> default <code>200</code>; <code>pinMigrationEase(s)</code> default <code>'power2.out'</code>. MultiWays AdjustPhase only.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Symbols and fill</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>symbols(cb)</code> required; the registry is created eagerly and <code>cb</code> runs immediately.</li>
        <li><code>weights(record)</code> default <code>{'{}'}</code>; unset ids weigh <code>10</code>.</li>
        <li><code>randomSymbols(pool, scope)</code>; buffer pools layer on top of spinning pools.</li>
        <li><code>symbolData(overrides)</code> merges across calls: <code>zIndex</code>, <code>unmask</code>, <code>weight</code>, <code>size</code>. Guards against v1 <code>size</code> keys.</li>
        <li><code>rng(fn)</code> default <code>Math.random</code>; <code>poolCapacity(n)</code> default derived.</li>
        <li><code>initialFrame(ColumnTarget[])</code> stored raw, validated at build.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mt-4 mb-1">Spin pipeline and infrastructure</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>spinningMode(mode)</code> default <code>new StandardMode()</code>; <code>frameMiddleware(mw)</code> pushed in call order.</li>
        <li><code>phases(cb)</code> deferred to the end of <code>build()</code>; <code>tumble(config?)</code> flips the default spin mode to <code>'cascade'</code>.</li>
        <li><code>ticker(t)</code> required; <code>gsap(instance)</code> per set, read at <code>build()</code>.</li>
        <li><code>visibleRows</code>, <code>visibleRowsPerReel</code>, <code>reelPixelHeights</code> are v1 stubs that always throw with a rename message.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Most setters only record a value, but a handful check their argument on
    the spot instead of waiting for <code>build()</code>, because a bad value
    there is unambiguous and the earlier error is more useful. Those are <code>curveMode</code>, <code>curveBleed</code>, <code>curveFocus</code>, <code>maskStrategy</code>, <code>motionModel</code>, <code>symbolZIndex</code>, <code>tumble</code>, <code>initialFrame</code> and the guards that catch v1 option names.
  </p>

  <h2>What _validate() can say</h2>

  <p>
    When step 1 throws, the message is this header followed by one line per
    problem. The lines appear in check order, so a long list is worth reading
    from the top: an early mistake (no reels, say) often explains the later
    ones.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`reels() must be called with a positive number.
one of visibleCells(n) or visibleCellsPerReel([...]) or multiways({...}) must be called.
cannot call both visibleCells() and visibleCellsPerReel(). pick one.
cannot combine multiways() with visibleCellsPerReel(). MultiWays shapes are server-driven.
visibleCellsPerReel length X must equal reels(N).
visibleCellsPerReel[i] = v must be positive.                 (first bad entry only)
reelExtents length X must equal reels(N).
multiways({minCells, maxCells}) must both be positive.
multiways: minCells a cannot exceed maxCells b.
multiways({reelExtent}) must be positive.
cannot combine multiways({reelExtent}) with reelExtents([...]) ...
big symbol 'id' (size RxC) cannot be registered on a MultiWays slot...   (first only)
big symbol 'id' (size RxC) must have weight 0. big symbols are placed by the server at anchor cells only...
big symbol 'id' spans R reels, which is not supported together with mixed directionPerReel([...])...
visibleCells() must be called with a positive number.
symbolSize() must be called with width and height.
symbols() must register at least one symbol.
ticker() must be called with a PixiJS Ticker.
initialSpeed 'x' does not match any registered speed profile. Available: ...   (only when a speed is registered)`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The four checks from step 2 each throw on their own, with their own
    message: <code>directionPerReel() length (a) must equal reels() (n).</code>, <code>curveMode('warp') renders each reel to a texture, so it needs a renderer: add .renderer(app.renderer)...</code>, <code>curvePerReel() length (a) must equal reels() (n).</code> and <code>bufferSymbols({'{ end: 0 }'}) is tumble-only...</code>.
  </p>

