  <h2>The closure rule</h2>

  <p>
    Step 19 looks like busywork: it copies a dozen builder fields into local
    constants before the reel loop runs. The reason is that the reel-making
    function outlives <code>build()</code>. The ReelSet keeps it inside the <code>ReelFactory</code> and calls it again whenever reels are added at
    runtime, which may be minutes later. If that function read the builder's
    fields directly, a builder you had since reused for a second set could
    change the way the first set builds its new reels. The source comment
    puts it plainly: a builder reused for another set must not reach into
    this one.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    Copying the fields makes <code>build()</code> a snapshot. Per-reel arrays
    are copied too, and <code>perReel()</code> reuses the last entry for any
    reel added past the array's end, so a set that grows keeps the style of
    its last column. The gsap instance is read at <code>build()</code> for the
    same reason: it is per set, where v1 used a module global.
  </Callout>

